--- title The datatype actions
--- about An action is one operation with an implementation per datatype.
--- about What each entry pins is which datatypes an action takes and
--- about what it answers for each, because that is where they differ
--- about from ordinary natives and where the surprises are.

--- id actions/absolute-of-a-negative
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
abs -5
--- result
5

--- id actions/absolute-of-a-positive-changes-nothing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
abs 5
--- result
5

--- id actions/absolute-of-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
abs 0
--- result
0

--- id actions/absolute-of-negative-zero-stays-negative
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which is the one answer here nobody would predict. Negative
--- note zero is a value floating point has and arithmetic keeps, and
--- note ABS does not take the sign off it.
--- code
abs -0.0
--- result
-0.0

--- id actions/absolute-of-the-most-negative-whole-number-overflows
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note There is no positive counterpart, so the answer does not exist
--- note rather than wrapping to itself.
--- code
abs -9223372036854775808
--- error math overflow

--- id actions/absolute-of-a-pair
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold abs -1x-2
--- result
"1x2"

--- id actions/absolute-of-a-time
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
abs -10:00
--- result
10:00

--- id actions/complement-flips-every-bit
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which for a whole number is one less than the negation.
--- code
complement 5
--- result
-6

--- id actions/complement-of-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
complement 0
--- result
-1

--- id actions/complement-of-all-bits-set
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
complement -1
--- result
0

--- id actions/complement-of-a-logic-negates-it
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The same word for two jobs: bits for a number, truth for a
--- note logic.
--- code
complement true
--- result
#(false)

--- id actions/counting-a-position-from-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The z family counts from zero where the rest of the language
--- note counts from one, so atz b 1 is the second item. Both spellings
--- note are legal and mixing them is off by one.
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
mold atz b 1
--- result
"[2 3]"

--- id actions/position-zero-is-the-head
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
mold atz b 0
--- result
"[1 2 3]"

--- id actions/the-last-position-from-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
mold atz b 2
--- result
"[3]"

--- id actions/one-past-the-last-is-the-tail
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
mold atz b 3
--- result
"[]"

--- id actions/further-past-the-tail-clamps
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Rather than raising, which is why there is no out-of-range
--- note failure for it.
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
mold atz b 4
--- result
"[]"

--- id actions/a-negative-position-clamps-to-the-head
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
mold atz b -1
--- result
"[1 2 3]"

--- id actions/asking-a-position-from-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
indexz? b
--- result
0

--- id actions/asking-a-position-one-along
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
indexz? next b
--- result
1

--- id actions/asking-the-tails-position-from-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
indexz? tail b
--- result
3

--- id actions/an-empty-series-is-at-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: []
indexz? b
--- result
0

--- id actions/the-head-is-not-past-the-tail
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
past? b
--- result
#(false)

--- id actions/nor-is-the-tail-itself
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which makes PAST? false for every position an ordinary series
--- note can be at.
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
past? tail b
--- result
#(false)

--- id actions/swapping-two-positions
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
swap b next b
b
--- result
[2 1 3]

--- id actions/swapping-a-position-with-itself
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
swap b b
b
--- result
[1 2 3]

--- id actions/reflecting-an-objects-words
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note REFLECT is the general form of the -OF functions, so this is
--- note what WORDS-OF is built on.
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1 b: 2]
mold reflect o 'words
--- result
"[a b]"

--- id actions/reflecting-an-objects-values
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1 b: 2]
mold reflect o 'values
--- result
"[1 2]"

--- id actions/reflecting-a-field-an-object-has-not-got
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note None rather than an error, so a caller can ask about a field
--- note that may not apply.
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
reflect o 'spec
--- result
_

--- id actions/random-of-one-is-always-one
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The only value RANDOM can answer here, which is what makes it
--- note testable without a seed.
--- code
random 1
--- result
1

--- id actions/random-of-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
random 0
--- result
0

--- id actions/picking-from-zero-gives-the-first-item
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
pickz b 0
--- result
1

--- id actions/picking-the-last-item-from-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
pickz b 2
--- result
3

--- id actions/picking-one-past-the-last-gives-nothing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note None rather than an error, the same way PICK behaves past the
--- note end.
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
pickz b 3
--- result
_

--- id actions/picking-before-the-first-gives-nothing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
pickz b -1
--- result
_

--- id actions/absolute-refuses-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
abs "a"
--- error script expect-arg

--- id actions/absolute-refuses-nothing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
abs none
--- error script expect-arg

--- id actions/complement-refuses-a-decimal
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Bits are a whole-number idea, so the fractional case is
--- note refused rather than truncated.
--- code
complement 5.5
--- error script expect-arg

--- id actions/complement-refuses-nothing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
complement none
--- error script expect-arg

--- id actions/a-position-from-zero-must-be-a-number
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
atz b "x"
--- error script expect-arg

--- id actions/only-a-series-has-a-position-from-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
atz 5 1
--- error script expect-arg

--- id actions/only-a-series-can-be-asked-its-position
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
indexz? 5
--- error script expect-arg

--- id actions/only-a-series-can-be-past-its-tail
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
past? 5
--- error script expect-arg

--- id actions/only-a-series-can-be-swapped
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
swap 5 6
--- error script expect-arg

--- id actions/swapping-at-the-tail-changes-nothing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note There is nothing at the tail to exchange, so the call is a
--- note no-op rather than a failure.
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
swap tail b b
b
--- result
[1 2 3]

--- id actions/swapping-between-two-different-series
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Each takes the other's item, so this moves values between
--- note blocks rather than within one.
--- code
b: [1 2]
c: [3 4]
swap b c
mold reduce [b c]
--- result
"[[3 2] [1 4]]"

--- id actions/random-shuffles-a-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Not "pick one at random", which is /only. Given a series it
--- note reorders it, and the answer is the series.
--- code
b: [1]
mold random b
--- result
"[1]"

--- id actions/random-shuffles-a-string-too
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
random "a"
--- result
"a"
